<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Icon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An icon is a religious work of art, most often a painting on a wood panel in egg tempera, in the cultures of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Oriental Orthodox, Catholic and Lutheran churches. Its usual subjects are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, Mary, saints and angels, though the term also covers narrative scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and the lives of saints. Orthodox tradition holds that Christian images go back to the earliest days of the faith, while modern art history traces the tradition only to the 3rd century. The widespread destruction of images in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-iconoclasm">Byzantine Iconoclasm</a> of 726–842 settled the question of their use, and icons have since kept a great continuity of style and subject.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/icon.jpg" alt="Icon" width="330" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pvasiliadis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ladder_of_Divine_Ascent_Monastery_of_St_Catherine_Sinai_12th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1170 the city repelled a siege by the forces of Andrey Bogolyubsky of Vladimir-Suzdal, a victory later attributed to the miraculous <b>Icon</b> of the Sign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li></ul></section>
